# 0005. ADR before code, granular ADRs, one ADR per PR

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

> Decided by the owner on 2026-11-11.

## Context
Claude could start writing code for a feature before the decision behind it was recorded
and approved, and one PR could mix several decisions, which made review and history hard
to follow.

## Decision
- In any project with a codebase, every feature or major change starts with an ADR. No
  code, scaffolding or config for it until I approve the ADR (`Accepted`) and give the go.
- ADRs are granular: one decision per ADR. A feature that needs several decisions gets
  several ADRs.
- If B depends on A, A's ADR gets the lower number and is implemented first.
- One ADR, one PR: the PR carries the ADR and its implementation, nothing else.
- Minor changes (fix, docs, tweak) need no new ADR; they use the latest ADR's minor number
  (`commit-style`).

## Alternatives
- ADRs only for big decisions, written after the code: the record follows the code instead
  of steering it.
- One ADR per feature with many decisions inside: hard to supersede one part.

## Rationale
Approving the decision before any code is cheaper than reviewing code built on a wrong one.
Granular, ordered ADRs map one-to-one onto PRs, tags (`ADR-NNNN`) and commit subjects.

## Consequences
- Positive: every major change has an approved record; history reads in dependency order.
- Negative: an extra approval round before coding; more, smaller PRs.

## References
`adr-manager` and `github-flow` skills, [0003](0003-only-the-owner-merges-to-main.adr).
